Phase 2: Establish shared quantities, shader conversion and storage support
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Depends on Phase 1. Outcome: Existing owners can supply calibrated parameters and store/diagnose
physical lighting consistently; producers can migrate without introducing new lighting systems.

  ☐ Add narrowly scoped quantity/conversion contracts to existing lighting owners and bindings.
    - Identify units in CPU parameters, generated interfaces and shader contracts; keep composition
      roots thin and avoid general-purpose unit frameworks or new propagation infrastructure.
    - Supply block-light reference lux/selected response parameters through existing shader bindings.
      Do not transform the propagated field or rewrite engine light data on the CPU.
    - Provide one shared GLSL block conversion: B=max(legacyBlockRgb,0), m=maxComponent(B),
      correctedRgb=B*max(m-neutralFloor,0)/m for m>0, otherwise zero.
      blockIlluminance=correctedRgb*referenceLux; integrated block input is E/pi.
    - Separate block conversion from atmospheric illumination and apply any storage representation
      at the declared boundary. Prevent duplicated conversion or division by pi.

  ☐ Implement the selected storage representation through existing resource owners.
    - Implement the approved frame pre-exposure and fixed persistent encoding/decoding contract.
      Account for physical quantities and frame/cache/lookup storage scales in typed interfaces
      and diagnostics.
    - Follow the required temporal/cache/water/LUT handling, freeze the frame scale before draws,
      and verify scale transitions before integrating producers.
    - Preserve engine framebuffer ownership, demand loading, reload, resize and world teardown.
    - Integrate the approved water-source refinement in the existing signed-boundary evaluator:
      min(max(sourceIntegral,0),sourceUpper*opticalDepth) times the retained integral factor.
      Publish a bound covering every contributing boundary and initial camera source through
      existing bindings; do not substitute receiver-local illumination. Reject unsupported
      bound metadata/products through the existing transport validity/fallback path.
      Preserve homogeneous response, transmission, medium coefficients and resource ownership.
      Do not introduce per-frame source discovery or reallocation for calibration.

  ☐ Add proportionate numerical and shader-contract validation.
    - Check normalized RGB Y versus scalar intensity, zero/invalid inputs and stored-unit round trips.
    - Check block conversion monotonicity, zero preservation and independence from exposure.
    - Compile affected shader contracts/variants using the existing catalog and installed sources.
    - Expose physical quantities/storage scale and active artistic departures through existing
      diagnostics without leaving temporary investigation logging in production.

  ☐ Verify completion of Phase 2.
    - Shared bindings/helper and selected storage support pass stated numerical/precision checks.
    - No new base-game lighting logic patch exists; standalone operation needs no LumOn resources.
    - All production/test methods and classes follow repository comments/layout conventions.

Phase 3: Calibrate lighting producers and normalize receiver response
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Depends on Phase 2. Outcome: Atmosphere, block field, punctual lights and emission produce the
declared quantities and use consistent receiver response in deferred and forward paths.

  ☐ Anchor atmosphere amplitude to the confirmed reference through existing model/lookup owners.
    - Derive solar amplitude once for the reference condition; preserve extinction, horizon/weather
      behavior and chromaticity. Do not normalize anew as the sun moves or weather changes.
    - Scale solar irradiance, sky luminance, integrated sky E/pi, disk and aerial in-scattering together.
      Keep attenuation dimensionless and direct sunlight out of the scattered-sky environment term.
    - Verify reference lux, the disk's integrated solar contribution and solar-elevation/weather cases.

  ☐ Convert propagated block illumination at existing VGE shader publication/evaluation points.
    - Use the shared helper in PbrTerrainColorPatches environment publication and applicable
      PbrSurfaceShaderPatches/forward routes before material response.
    - Bind the confirmed reference/curve consistently; preserve atmospheric contribution separately.
      Decode dimensionless sky availability with active-table, decoder-specific zero/full
      endpoints; terrain's encoded level-zero alpha and the world accessor differ.
      Reuse the Surface Cache's normalized light RGBA input for the same block conversion;
      retire its differently decoded legacy level/32 palette route.
    - Preserve generation, propagation, attenuation, enclosure, interpolation and overlap behavior.
      Confirm raw engine data is unchanged and no source identity/direction is fabricated.
    - Verify each contribution is converted once and fixture outputs match the selected mapping.

  ☐ Convert punctual lights and material emission without relabeling legacy payloads.
    - Author VGE punctual output in lumens, convert to evaluated intensity, and use metre-based
      receiver illumination with the documented distance regularization/influence window.
    - Apply the selected compatibility mapping to engine/held payloads; resolve double-counting
      identified in Phase 1 without adding source discovery or replacing block propagation.
    - Convert dimensionless emissive coefficients to declared RGB luminance/reference nits.
      Preserve authoring compatibility and separate glow eligibility from emitted brightness.

  ☐ Unify direct and integrated-environment receiver normalization.
    - Update pbr_direct_lighting, pbr_forward_surface and shared BRDF/environment helpers so
      converted sun and punctual inputs receive consistent diffuse normalization.
    - Remove the legacy point-light omission of pi only alongside its converted input contract.
    - Preserve Fresnel/metallic energy partition, transmission and shadow ownership; validate units.
    - Keep indirect AO on environment lighting and direct visibility on sun/point lighting.
    - Implement the qualified solar-only finite-angular specular response in the shared deferred/
      forward direct-light boundary and separate pbr_liquid solar-reflection call. Preserve punctual response,
      calibrated solar irradiance, existing shadows and volume-scattering/transmission contracts.

  ☐ Verify completion of Phase 3.
    - Numerical diffuse-reference and installed deferred/forward tests meet stated tolerances.
    - A synthetic isotropic 1,000-lumen source matches one-/two-metre expected illumination
      outside regularization; doubling distance quarters illumination.
    - Atmosphere/source-isolation/block/emission tests pass without exposure compensation masking
      source-ratio errors. Unsupported physical claims about engine propagation are absent.

Phase 4: Reconcile LumOn and all scene-lighting consumers
-------------------------------------------------------
Depends on Phase 3. Outcome: Both lighting modes and other scene contributors use compatible
quantities without double material weighting, duplicate environment light or unscaled effects.

  ☐ Apply the audited quantity contract at LumOn capture, probe, gather and composite boundaries.
    - Trace real consumers and explicitly convert outgoing luminance versus incident estimates
      where required. Do not infer compatible units from RGB format or variable naming alone.
    - Apply receiver response and calibration exactly once; preserve justified transport steps.
      Convert world-probe diffuse incident lux to E/pi at the shared gather fallback boundary;
      retain its separate directional-radiance route. Verify constant-input screen/world agreement.
    - Remove hidden calibration scales or classify them as artistic controls with neutral defaults.
    - Prevent re-addition of environment light represented by GI; retain the established unavailable-
      GI behavior and forward local block fallback, with no sampling of unrelated opaque receivers.

  ☐ Reconcile remaining scene contributions with existing owners and publication boundaries.
    - Cover water reflection/refraction, underwater in-scattering, transparent/late and held
      receivers, particles, fog, celestial inputs and glare.
    - Convert legacy effect inputs explicitly; document remaining approximations instead of
      injecting unscaled display colors into physical scene buffers.
    - Follow proposal "Additive compatibility contributors": use the selected 32-nit reference
      only for authored/prelit approximations, publish expression/input/submission bounds through
      existing owners, and include their actual contributions in B. Keep physical lighting separate.
      Disable volumetric-cloud post-traversal RGBA display noise on scene draws under
      PBR.OutputDithering.md; preserve final encoded dithering and offscreen behavior.
    - Preserve UI/offscreen isolation and existing alpha, depth, glow and material-data meanings.

  ☐ Retire differently calibrated cached/history lighting through existing lifecycle owners.
    - Invalidate dependent data when calibration or storage conventions change; implement any
      temporal rescaling required by the selected storage design.
    - Verify resize, shader reload, world teardown, lighting-mode changes and calibration changes
      cannot mix stale and current quantities or expose incomplete publications.

  ☐ Verify completion of Phase 4.
    - Deterministic isolated/combined-source tests cover LumOn on/off, fallback and unavailable-GI cases.
    - Material weighting, source calibration and indirect AO occur once at their declared boundaries.
    - Other scene inputs and persistent data meet unit, finite-value and lifecycle contracts.

Phase 5: Integrate camera exposure, glare and configuration compatibility
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Depends on Phase 4. Outcome: Physical lighting reaches the display through a coherent camera
response, and existing settings/material/source inputs have documented migration behavior.

  ☐ Rebase existing exposure/metering to the selected physical/storage scale.
    - Update histogram bounds, accepted range and exposure limits from the qualified scene envelope.
    - Keep stops as log2 display multiplier and compensation separate from physical source output.
      Do not introduce or claim EV100 camera calibration.
    - Preserve metering after scene/OIT/late composition and before generated glare/display conversion.
      Decode the frozen frame scale for metering and apply currentExposure/P at final display.
    - Retain the existing global tone curve initially; apply display exposure once and keep UI outside it.

  ☐ Reconcile bloom/shaft thresholds and artistic controls.
    - State whether thresholds are physical or exposed quantities and convert consistently.
    - Prevent generated bloom/shafts feeding back into metering or camera exposure changing source
      power, emission, AO, material data or propagation.
    - Verify fixed-exposure ratios first, then automatic adaptation and existing display grading.

  ☐ Version and migrate settings and material/source mappings through existing configuration owners.
    - Specify finite defaults and migration for block calibration, source/emission mappings and
      exposure values whose previous scene-relative interpretation no longer applies.
    - Preserve existing saves and third-party input compatibility through explicit conversions.
      Do not promise exact reproduction of uncalibrated old screenshots.
    - Remove superseded conversion paths and temporary instrumentation after replacement is
      verified. Retain intentional compatibility adapters and document their limits.

  ☐ Verify completion of Phase 5.
    - Source ratios, metering, display conversion and glare behavior pass independent checks.
    - Migration/default/invalid-input tests cover persisted settings and compatible engine sources.
    - No mixed-scale cache state, duplicate exposure or camera-to-source feedback remains.

Phase 6: Qualify the integrated candidate and reconcile documentation
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Depends on Phase 5. Outcome: A reviewable candidate has numerical, integration, package and
live-runtime evidence; accepted defaults and approximation limits are documented.

  ☐ Run focused and required integrated validation through subagents.
    - Validate unit arithmetic, fixture agreement, generated/installed shader contracts and
      both lighting modes. Expand testing only for actual changed contracts or unresolved failures.
    - Qualify accepted HDR extremes and precision, scale changes, configuration migrations,
      resource lifecycle and complete scene/display routing against the fixed tolerances.
    - Record build/settings, source/candidate provenance and numerical results. Analytical,
      texture-quantization and approximate-transport tolerances remain separately reported.

  ☐ Package an isolated candidate for user-run reference and appearance checks.
    - Record package identity and tested-source correspondence; exclude fixture/debug instrumentation.
      Do not install or launch the game for visual acceptance.
    - Obtain fixed-exposure neutral-grading checks of isolated and combined sun, sky and lamp sources.
    - Obtain automatic-exposure checks of lamp-versus-sun balance, indoor/outdoor transitions,
      night visibility, transparent/held surfaces, water and LumOn enabled/disabled.
    - Reconcile observed problems with numerical receipts; do not use automatic exposure or stronger
      AO as a substitute for calibration. Record user acceptance and actual coverage honestly.

  ☐ Update linked documentation to describe the retained implementation.
    - Reconcile the proposal, PBR.MaterialColorAndDisplay.md, PBR.LightingModes.md,
      PBR.CameraExposure.md, PBR.SharedDisplay.md and PBR.Postprocessing.md with verified behavior.
    - Document units, references, spatial conversions, storage representation, settings migration,
      neutral artistic defaults and aggregate-field/transport limitations in the appropriate owners.
    - Update baseline planning references/status only after linked scope and evidence agree.
      Do not treat the absence of previously referenced documentation as proof of completion.

  ☐ Review the complete implementation and evidence against the proposal before marking complete.
    - Map each proposal requirement to retained code, tests, documentation or its explicit exclusion.
    - Check shader-only block conversion and the exclusion of engine logic patches/new lighting systems.
    - Resolve remaining discrepancies; distinguish headless numerical/package receipts from live
      appearance acceptance and avoid unsupported physical/performance claims.

Final acceptance
----------------
  ☐ All calibration prerequisites and selected storage qualification are resolved in the governing design.
  ☐ Sun, sky, block, point, emission, LumOn and other scene contributions share declared quantities.
  ☐ Existing propagation/light data, render ownership and UI/offscreen isolation remain intact.
  ☐ Required numerical, shader, integration, lifecycle, migration and package checks pass.
  ☐ User-run appearance/adaptation acceptance and its actual coverage are recorded.
  ☐ Linked documents, cleanup and final review agree with the retained implementation and scope.
